## Cause

The issue stems from `this`. When a regular `function` is provided to `setInterval`, `this` does not point to the `Timer` object. Instead, it references the global object (or is `undefined` if in strict mode). Consequently, `this.seconds` becomes `undefined`, and adding 1 to `undefined` produces `NaN`.

## Fix

Replace the regular function with an arrow function. Arrow functions lack their own `this` binding — instead, they inherit `this` from the enclosing scope, which in this context is the `Timer` instance.

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

With this approach, `this` remains connected to the `Timer` instance, allowing `this.seconds` to increment properly with each interval execution.

